---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
import * as CH from "./gdp.charts.js";
const NUM = CH.NUM;
---
<Learn
  title="GDP: what it counts, what it misses · Measure of India"
  description="What GDP actually is: the value of everything produced, minus what was used up making it. What a decade of real GDP honestly measures, and the three big things it cannot see."
  slug="gdp"
  kicker="Growth: GDP"
  h1="GDP counts the output. <i>It cannot count who gets it.</i>"
  lede="The value of everything produced in the country, minus what was used up producing it: what GDP honestly measures, and the three big things it cannot see."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "GDP"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Real GDP, Q1 FY17 to Q2 FY26"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "9 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="GDP"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Every quarter, the government's statisticians add up the value of everything produced in the country: steel and software, wheat and haircuts, bank credit and bus rides. Then they subtract what was used up in the producing, the mill's iron ore, the cloud the software firm rents. What remains is GDP. It is the largest single number in Indian statistics, and the most argued over.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">GDP <span class="sub">gross domestic product, a flow, quarterly</span></div>
          <p>The value of all final goods and services produced within the country in a period, minus the value of the inputs used up making them. Growth of 8.23 per cent means output was that much larger than in the same quarter a year earlier, with prices held constant.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the size of the economy's engine, measured every three months.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">Two words in that definition do the heavy work. <b>Final</b> means the bread is counted and the flour is not; counting both would count the flour twice. And <b>produced</b> means the sale of an old house adds nothing to the total; only the broker's service is new. What each producer contributes has its own name.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Value added <span class="sub">output minus inputs</span></div>
          <p>What each producer contributes: the value of what leaves the gate minus the value of what came in. A bakery buys flour for ₹40 and sells bread for ₹100; its value added is ₹60. GDP is the sum of every producer's value added, which is why nothing is counted twice.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> each firm reports only what it itself made.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">One more property matters, and it is the most forgotten. GDP is a flow, not a stock. It measures the speed of the river, not the depth of the lake. There is no reservoir of past GDP to draw on when work stops: the quarter the economy stopped, the number simply collapsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>A flow, not a stock.</b> Wealth is the lake; GDP is the river. One bad quarter cannot be paid for out of good ones.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Final goods only.</b> The flour is already inside the bread's price. Count it again and the economy looks bigger without producing more.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Real, not nominal.</b> The chart on this page holds prices at 2011-12 levels. What grows is volume, not the price tag.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : a decade of growth -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>A decade, drawn once</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, quarterly</span></div>
    <p>Drawn as a growth rate, the decade looks like a heart monitor: a steady pulse near 6 per cent, one arrest, a rebound off the fallen base, then a return to rhythm. The dotted rule is the rough pre-pandemic trend, drawn as a reference, not a target.</p>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-gdp" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Thirty-eight quarters of growth: and the one the economy stopped</h3>
            <div class="sub">Real GDP, year-on-year, constant 2011-12 prices, Q1 FY17 to Q2 FY26</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-gdp" set:html={NUM["pv-gdp"]}></div><div class="c">Q2 FY26, GDP y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-gdp" set:html={CH.gdp()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, National Accounts, base 2011-12, vintage <span id="pf-gdp" set:html={NUM["pf-gdp"]}></span>, dotted: rough 6% pre-pandemic trend</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Every point compares one quarter with the same quarter a year earlier. Above zero, output grew; below zero, it shrank. Read the distance from the dotted rule, not only the sign: 3 per cent is still growth, but it is half the old rhythm.</p>
            <p>The crash and the rebound are a pair. Output fell by nearly a quarter in the lockdown, then rose by nearly a quarter, because it was measured against the wreckage. Neither figure is a lie. Both are arithmetic, and the arithmetic has a name: the base effect.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The FY26 case</span>
            <p>The last four quarters read <b id="fy26-run" set:html={NUM["fy26-run"]}></b>: every one above the trend rule, ending at <b id="fy26-last" set:html={NUM["fy26-last"]}></b> in Q2 FY26. Runs above the rule have come in bursts in this series. FY24 was one such burst; the last four quarters are another.</p>
            <p>Notice the difference from the rebound. The 2021 spike stood on a destroyed base. This run stands on ordinary quarters, which is what makes it worth reading slowly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 what it cannot see -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>What the number cannot see</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Three blind spots</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The print, Q2 FY26</div><div class="v acc" id="n-last" set:html={NUM["n-last"]}></div><div class="d">real GDP, year-on-year</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The quarter before</div><div class="v" id="n-prev" set:html={NUM["n-prev"]}></div><div class="d">Q1 FY26, same measure</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Pre-pandemic average</div><div class="v" id="n-trend" set:html={NUM["n-trend"]}></div><div class="d">FY17 to FY20, the dotted rule</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The lockdown quarter</div><div class="v" id="n-crash" set:html={NUM["n-crash"]}></div><div class="d">Q1 FY21, the flow stopped</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">For one number, GDP is honest about what it is. The misuse begins when it is asked questions it cannot answer. Three of them matter most.</p>
    <p><b>Distribution, first.</b> GDP is a total; it cannot say who received it. If all of a year's extra output accrues to a thin slice of households, the print still rises, and the median family sees nothing. The aggregate carries no map of its own spread.</p>
    <p><b>The informal economy, second.</b> Most Indians work outside the formal sector, in activities the statisticians cannot observe directly and must estimate by proxy: crop forecasts, household surveys, ratios carried over from the formal firms. How well those proxies tracked the informal sector after the pandemic, we do not know. Honest readings of GDP hold that doubt in view.</p>
    <p><b>Unpaid work, third.</b> Cooking, cleaning and care inside a household produce real value every day. No money changes hands, so the accounts record none of it. A meal cooked at home is invisible; the same meal bought in a restaurant raises GDP.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“GDP growth means everyone is getting richer.”</h3>
          <p>No. GDP is a total, and a total cannot see its own spread. Growth can pool at the top while most incomes stand still, and the print will not say so. The question to ask of any GDP figure is a second question: <b>whose incomes grew?</b> This number does not carry the answer.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“GDP measures how well people live.”</h3>
          <p>It measures production, not welfare. A town rebuilt after a flood adds to GDP without leaving anyone better off than before the flood. Health, leisure and clean air move lives without moving the total. Use GDP for the question it answers: <b>how much was produced?</b></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“8% growth means my income grew 8%.”</h3>
          <p>The 8.23 per cent is an average over 1.4 billion people and every sector at once. Any single income can sit far above or far below it. Population grows too, so per person the figure is smaller. <b>The aggregate is not a payslip.</b></p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Quarterly estimates of GDP at constant 2011-12 prices; year-on-year growth computed from the published levels, vintage 2026-08-04. Quarters of the past two years are provisional estimates and will be revised.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>GDP here means real GDP, the headline market-price measure, compared year-on-year. Nominal GDP, which carries price changes inside it, is a different series and grows faster when inflation runs high.</p>
    <p>Where the accounts cannot see, the informal sector above all, this page says so rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Growth, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. The FY26 episode is used as history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/gdp/">National Accounts</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
